# Building a Simple Terminal-Based Operating System

## High-Level OS Architecture

```text
+--------------------------------------------------+
|                User Programs                     |
|        (shell, ls, cat, echo, etc.)              |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|                 Shell / Terminal                 |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|             System Call Interface                |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|                     Kernel                       |
|--------------------------------------------------|
|  • Process Manager                               |
|  • Memory Manager                                |
|  • File System                                   |
|  • Device Drivers                                |
|  • IPC Manager                                   |
|  • Scheduler                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|             Hardware / Emulator                  |
+--------------------------------------------------+
```

---

# Operating System Development Roadmap

The process of creating a terminal-based operating system typically follows this order:

1. Bootloader
2. Enter Protected / Long Mode
3. Kernel Initialization
4. Terminal Output
5. Interrupt Handling
6. Memory Management
7. Process / Task System
8. Keyboard Input
9. Shell
10. File System
11. User Programs
12. Advanced Features

---

# Stage 1 — Development Environment

You generally do **not** develop directly on real hardware initially.

Most OS developers use:

* Cross-compilers
* Emulators / Virtual Machines

---

## Required Tools

### Compiler

Most commonly:

```bash
i386-elf-gcc
```

Used for compiling 32-bit x86 kernels.

---

### Emulator

Most common choice:

```bash
qemu-system-i386
```

This safely runs your operating system inside a virtual machine.

---

### Build Tools

Common tools:

* Make
* NASM
* LD Linker

---

# Stage 2 — Bootloader

When the CPU powers on, it starts in a very primitive state.

The bootloader is responsible for:

* Loading the kernel into memory
* Switching CPU modes
* Jumping execution to the kernel

---

## Option A — Use GRUB (Recommended)

GRUB simplifies booting significantly.

### Boot Flow

```text
BIOS / UEFI
      ↓
     GRUB
      ↓
    Kernel
```

You create:

* Kernel binary
* GRUB configuration
* Bootable ISO image

---

## Option B — Write Your Own Bootloader

More educational, but significantly harder.

Requires:

* Assembly programming
* BIOS interaction
* Manual sector loading

---

# Stage 3 — Enter Protected Mode (i386)

The CPU initially starts in:

```text
16-bit Real Mode
```

Modern kernels switch into:

```text
32-bit Protected Mode
```

Later systems may use:

```text
64-bit Long Mode
```

---

## Benefits of Protected Mode

* Memory protection
* 32-bit registers
* Paging support
* Multitasking capabilities

---

# Stage 4 — Minimal Kernel

Your first kernel usually just prints text.

Example:

```c
void kernel_main() {
    print("Hello from kernel!");
}
```

---

## VGA Text Mode

Most beginner kernels write directly to VGA memory:

```c
char* video = (char*) 0xB8000;
```

This displays text directly to the screen.

---

# Stage 5 — Interrupts

Interrupts allow hardware and exceptions to notify the CPU.

Examples:

* Keyboard presses
* Divide-by-zero exceptions
* Timer ticks

---

## Interrupt Descriptor Table (IDT)

You will create:

* Interrupt handlers
* Interrupt tables
* ISR assembly stubs

### Interrupt Flow

```text
Hardware
    ↓
Interrupt Controller
    ↓
       IDT
    ↓
Kernel Handler
```

---

# Stage 6 — Memory Management

One of the most important parts of an OS.

---

## Physical Memory Manager (PMM)

Tracks:

* Free RAM pages
* Used RAM pages

Often implemented with:

* Bitmap allocators

---

## Virtual Memory Manager (VMM)

Maps:

```text
Virtual Addresses → Physical Addresses
```

Uses:

* Paging
* Page tables

### Benefits

* Isolation
* Security
* Process independence

---

# Stage 7 — Scheduler & Processes

At this point your OS can run multiple tasks.

---

## Process Control Block (PCB)

Each process stores metadata such as:

```text
PID
Registers
Stack
State
Memory Map
Priority
```

---

## Scheduler

Determines which process runs next.

### Beginner Scheduler

* Round Robin

### Scheduling Flow

```text
Timer Interrupt
        ↓
    Scheduler
        ↓
  Context Switch
        ↓
   Next Process
```

---

## Context Switching

The OS saves:

* Registers
* Stack pointer
* Instruction pointer

Then restores another process state.

---

# Stage 8 — Keyboard Driver

Required for terminal input.

---

## PS/2 Keyboard

Read scan codes from:

```text
Port 0x60
```

Convert:

```text
Scan Code → ASCII
```

Then pass input into the shell.

---

# Stage 9 — Building the Shell

Your OS is now interactive.

Example prompt:

```text
MyOS>
```

---

## Shell Responsibilities

The shell:

* Reads keyboard input
* Parses commands
* Launches programs
* Interacts with kernel APIs

---

## Example Commands

```text
help
clear
echo
ls
reboot
meminfo
```

---

# Stage 10 — File System

Without storage, your OS loses all data after shutdown.

---

## Beginner File Systems

Good starter options:

* FAT12
* FAT16
* Simple custom filesystem

---

## File System Architecture

```text
Shell
  ↓
VFS (Optional)
  ↓
Filesystem Driver
  ↓
Disk Driver
  ↓
Hardware
```

---

# Stage 11 — User Mode

Initially everything runs in kernel mode.

Later you add:

* User processes
* Privilege separation

---

## x86 Ring Levels

```text
Ring 0 → Kernel
Ring 3 → User Applications
```

System calls provide safe transitions between them.

---

# Stage 12 — System Calls

Applications need controlled access to kernel functionality.

Example system calls:

```c
write()
read()
fork()
exec()
open()
```

### System Call Flow

```text
User Program
      ↓
System Call Interface
      ↓
     Kernel
```

---

# Stage 13 — IPC (Inter-Process Communication)

Processes need communication mechanisms.

Common IPC methods:

* Pipes
* Shared memory
* Message queues
* Signals

---

# Stage 14 — Device Drivers

Drivers allow communication with hardware.

Examples:

* Keyboard
* Timer
* Disk
* Serial Port

Drivers are often highly hardware-specific.

---

# Stage 15 — Advanced Features

After the basics are functional:

---

## Networking

* Ethernet
* TCP/IP stack
* Sockets

---

## Advanced Multitasking

* Preemptive scheduling
* Priorities

---

## Security

* Permissions
* User accounts

---

## ELF Executables

Run compiled user programs.

---

## Dynamic Memory

Kernel heap allocator.

---

## SMP (Symmetric Multiprocessing)

Support for multiple CPU cores.

---

